{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## 9a17 FIX VERIFIED ON MAIN -- all four pytest cells green at the fix commit\n\nBranch-head run 33840948374, headSha 668523feffde. This is the first post-fix branch-head result and\nit is the confirmation the close was betting on:\n  ubuntu py3.11 / py3.12 / py3.13 and macos py3.13 -- ALL SUCCESS.\npy3.13 is the cell that was failing pre-fix. Every sweep tier green except the known-informational\nWindows one.\n\nThe run still concludes 'failure', and it is worth being precise about why, because 'main is red' and\n'main is broken' are no longer the same statement. Exactly two jobs fail:\n - scanner-integration (90c7, another session's, fails on main itself) -- the SOLE cause of the red\n   conclusion.\n - sweep (windows) (f850) -- continue-on-error, cannot affect a conclusion, has never passed.\n\nSo main's red is now attributable to one owned blocker rather than a tangle of unknowns, and the\ndefect that was failing ~46% of runs repo-wide is genuinely gone. That is the primary job in a good\nstate as far as it is within my control.\n\nNote the monitor's new filter did exactly its job on its first real firing: it labelled this\nFAILED(post-fix, ACTIONABLE) and I investigated immediately, where the previous four events were\ncorrectly suppressed as pre-fix noise. The signal-to-noise fix paid for itself within one cycle."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788503148818030001,"uuid":"17fe3b0f-c620-4b8c-85e0-6584441a0cff"}